  - graph theory
  - Python
  - C#
abstract: >-
  Triordinal continues DTree (versions 1.0.0 to 1.8.0, doi:10.5281/zenodo.23395710).
  DTree encodes the topology and structure of single-rooted directed trees
  explicitly, by two linear projections Q1 and Q2: node u reaches node v if
  and only if u strictly precedes v in both. A classical tree answers such
  questions by walking up parent links—a blind date that only shows the next
  step. DTree navigates toward the target: two positions tell at once whether
  it is reachable, independent of depth. The encoding is updated
  incrementally: a new node goes to positions known in advance, and rational
  (mediant) positions mean that inserting a node normally moves no other
  node. Beyond single pairs, and/or queries on the topology (does u reach all
  or some of a set; is v reached by all or some) are answered with
  constructed nodes. Python and C# implementations (PyPI: triordinal-dtree,
  NuGet: Triordinal.DTree, both on the shared list triordinal-dlist) support insertion, deletion, subtree moves, path
  queries and node handles; a WPF viewer is included.
